# ── Ops output that lives inside the tree by design (SEC-B47) ────────────────────────────────
# compose bind-mounts `./backup` into the backup sidecar, so snapshots land in the repository working
# directory. `*.sqlite` above matches neither `*.sqlite.gz` (what the script writes) nor `.CORRUPT`
# (integrity failures kept for forensics), so `git add -A` during an incident — exactly when an operator
# types it — would commit a gzip of production data (wallets, IP networks, device hashes, payment rows).
# The whole directory, not just the default `out/` subdirectory: an operator who sets OUT_DIR=/backup (or
# an older deployment that did) would otherwise be left with the same hole one level up. `.gitkeep` is the
# placeholder compose binds against and stays tracked; `ops/deploy/secrets/` has its own nested ignore.
ops/deploy/backup/**
!ops/deploy/backup/.gitkeep
# ── Secrets: by convention, not by luck (docs/09 §1.4, docs/07 §1.1) ─────────────────────────
# `backend/.env` was the only env file ignored, so a `client/.env.local` with a DSN or a key, or a
# `backend/.env.prod`, was one `git add -A` away from the public repo. Same for the cold keypairs
# `npm run program-ids -- new` writes: the ceremony is worthless if step 2 is "commit them by habit".
.env
.env.*
!**/.env.example
!**/.env.test
*-keypair.json
!tests/localnet/fixtures/sb_mock-keypair.json
keys/
*.pem
*.p12
# id_ed25519-style names sometimes land in a repo root during a manual deploy
.deploy*/

